{"text": "Start with the problem. To magnify a distant subject, a lens needs a long focal length. Put simply, the light needs room to travel after it passes through the glass, before it comes into focus. More zoom, more distance. That's why sports photographers carry lenses the size of a forearm. A phone's telephoto camera needs more than a centimetre and a half of travel. Stack that on top of the lens and the sensor, and it simply doesn't fit inside the phone. So engineers stopped trying to go deep, and went sideways instead. Beneath the lens sits a single slab of glass, called a tetraprism. Watch the ray. It drops in through the top, and hits an angled, mirror-coated face. One. It shoots across and glances off the top surface, where it's trapped by total internal reflection. Two. Off the bottom. Three. Off a second angled mirror. Four. Only then does it drop out of the glass, and onto the sensor. Think of it like this. You need a runner to cover a long hallway, but all you have is a closet. So you line the closet with mirrors, and let the runner zigzag from wall to wall, until they've covered the full distance. Same journey, a fraction of the space. The light has no idea it was folded. It arrives having travelled much farther than the phone is thick. One more trick. To focus, something has to move, and in a camera this cramped, there's no room to slide heavy glass back and forth. So the sensor itself moves, nudged by tiny electromagnets, in steps measured in microns, thousandths of a millimetre, until the image snaps sharp.", "alignment": {"characters": ["S", "t", "a", "r", "t", " ", "w", "i", "t", "h", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "p", "r", "o", "b", "l", "e", "m", ".", " ", "T", "o", " ", "m", "a", "g", "n", "i", "f", "y", " ", "a", " ", "d", "i", "s", "t", "a", "n", "t", " ", "s", "u", "b", "j", "e", "c", "t", ",", " ", "a", " ", "l", "e", "n", "s", " ", "n", "e", "e", "d", "s", " ", "a", " ", "l", "o", "n", "g", " ", "f", "o", "c", "a", "l", " ", "l", "e", "n", "g